    "created_at": "2026-05-27T22:29:41Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-05-27T22:29:41Z",
    "body": "bumping this!\n\ncontext: a Chromium bug (crbug.com/478905599) and CL (https://chromium-review.googlesource.com/c/chromium/src/+/7510578) were just held pending spec clarification here.\n\ni see three options, and would like to hear what direction this might go.\n\n1. **Dedicated events (original proposal).** Add `posterload` / `postererror` fired from the poster-frame algorithm. Cleanly scoped, doesn't entangle the poster with `readyState` semantics, and matches the precedent of `<img>`'s `load`/`error`. This is the approach I would lean toward for spec'ing.\n\n2. **Reuse `loadedmetadata` for poster-only videos.** Reportedly matches what Firefox does today (per the Chromium reporter -- I haven't independently verified). Downside: `loadedmetadata` is defined against media-resource metadata (duration, tracks, dimensions of the *video*), and the poster's natural size is explicitly allowed to differ from the video's. Overloading it muddies the contract for sites that have a `src` *and* a `poster`.\n\n3. **Slotted `<img>` / `<picture>` for the poster.** Best long-term ergonomics, largest spec/impl surface, won't help the immediate \"I just want to know when the poster is up\" use case.\n\nA possible middle path: spec (1) now (small, additive, web-compat-friendly), and keep (3) on the table as the bigger story?\n\nHappy to put up a draft PR for option (1) if there's rough agreement, pretty new to the spec side of html, so I'm not 100% aware of all processes; please let me know what the best next steps would be.\n\ncc @whatwg/media @dalecurtis",
